                  <provenance type="#b"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_596">Windsor, Berkshire. Royal
                        collegiate chapel of St George</orgName><p>Medieval binding with the usual
                        Windsor chain-staple mark and characteristic endband stitching.</p><p>A
                        handsome MS from the same scriptorium as Auct. D. 2. 11.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_467">Robertsbridge,
                        Sussex, Cistercian abbey of St Mary the Virgin</orgName>
                     <p>This attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of
                        Great Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987). The
                        attribution would depend on the following transcription (per the
                           <title>Summary Catalogue</title>): <q>Cuiusdam sacerdotis Pontis[?]
                           Roberti et fuit de istis[?] monachis</q>. However, Neil Ker later added a
                        note to the card indicating his skepticism: <q><q>Pontis</q> very doubtful
                           [...] and <q>istis monachis</q> loks to me more like <q>Northfolchia</q>;
                           anyway only a scribble and no evidence for
                     provenance.</q></p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Given by Dean and Canons of Windsor
                     1612.</provenance>
               </history>
